---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 9: Apply CAM to a Practice Piece</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 3</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 9</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Apply CAM to a Practice Piece</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Sessions of Vocabulary</h2>
              <p>Profile, offset, pocket, step-over, step-down, tabs.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Today, all of it, programmed for real &mdash; on one small
                shape, not the real project yet.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Meet the Test Coupon</h2>
              <p>
                A small practice shape that includes a bit of everything:
                an outer boundary, an interior pocket, and a hole.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Meet the Test Coupon</h2>
              <p>
                A real, standard manufacturing practice &mdash; verify your
                setup on something small and cheap before committing to the
                real part.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Job Has Three Toolpaths</h2>
              <ol>
                <li class="fragment">Profile &mdash; cut the outer boundary free (Outside offset)</li>
                <li class="fragment">Pocket &mdash; clear the interior recess (step-over set)</li>
                <li class="fragment">Drill &mdash; the hole</li>
              </ol>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Order Matters</h2>
              <p>
                Cut the pocket and drill the hole <i>before</i> the profile
                cut frees the part &mdash; not after.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Order Matters</h2>
              <p>
                Once the profile cut separates the part (tabs aside), it's
                far less securely held for any further operation.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Simulate Before You Trust It</h2>
              <p>
                Run the simulation and actually look at it &mdash; check
                that all three operations ran, in the order you intended,
                without the toolpaths colliding with each other.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Sequence is part of the program, not an afterthought.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Which operation runs first can matter as much as getting
                each individual one right.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A simulation that looks right still needs a human check.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Don't just glance at it &mdash; confirm each toolpath did
                what you actually intended.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 10: Post-Processing &amp; Troubleshooting</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Turning this simulated job into real code your machine can
                run &mdash; and catching mistakes before they cost material.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Set Up the Job</h3>
          <p>
            Using the test coupon shape your instructor provides (or the one
            you design, per their instructions), create a CAM Studio Job and
            select the part and stock.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Program the Pocket</h3>
          <p>
            Set up a Pocket toolpath for the interior recess. Choose a
            step-over and step-down, and be ready to justify both.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Program the Drill</h3>
          <p>
            Add a Drill toolpath for the hole. First, do the check from
            Session 7: <b class="text-sn-theme">is this hole the same
            diameter as your bit?</b> The coupon's hole is sized so that it
            is &mdash; which is exactly why a Drill toolpath works here. If
            it weren't, this would have to be a pocket instead.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Program the Profile</h3>
          <p>
            Add a Profile toolpath for the outer boundary, using Outside
            offset. Confirm it's sequenced <b class="text-sn-theme">after</b>
            the pocket and drill operations.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 5: Simulate and Check</h3>
          <p>
            Run the simulation. Confirm all three toolpaths ran, in the
            right order, with no collisions or unexpected gouges.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Pocket and drill are both sequenced before the profile cut</li>
            <li>Your step-over and step-down choices are ones you can justify, not defaults left unexamined</li>
            <li>The simulation ran clean, and you actually watched it, not just glanced at the result</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Did you get the toolpath order right on your first attempt? If not, what tipped you off that something was wrong &mdash; and would you have caught it without simulating?</li>
            <li>Session 10 covers turning this simulation into real machine code. What do you think could still go wrong even after a completely clean simulation?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your CAM Studio file with all three toolpaths and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
